mmc: dw_mmc: do not spin for 500ms when the host reads back all-ones

dw_mci_wait_while_busy() polls SDMMC_STATUS with readl_poll_timeout_atomic() for
up to 500ms before every data command. The poll is atomic - no sleeping - and it
runs with host->lock held and interrupts disabled.

When the controller or card stops answering the register reads back 0xffffffff,
so SDMMC_STATUS_BUSY is permanently set and every command burns the full 500ms.
A driver that retries then pins the CPU indefinitely. Observed on a Gusgu H7
(RK3326) when the RK915 SDIO wifi chip drops off the bus under load:

    rcu: INFO: rcu_sched self-detected stall on CPU
    CPU: 0 UID: 0 PID: 491 Comm: rk915_tx
    pc : dw_mci_start_command+0x58/0x120
    x19: 00000000ffffffff
    Call trace:
     dw_mci_start_command+0x58/0x120 (P)
     ...
     sdio_memcpy_toio+0x20/0x28
     sdio_send_data+0x24/0x2c [rk915]
     tx_thread+0x24c/0x2c0 [rk915]

x19 is the status readback. Interrupts stop being serviced on that CPU, so the
display stalls waiting for vblank, the network dies, and the machine wedges hard
enough that printk stops coming out - only a power cycle recovers it. The wifi
driver never gets an error back either, so its own firmware error recovery is
never entered.

An all-ones read is not a valid status; it means the host is not there. Break out
of the poll and let the command time out normally so the error propagates.

--- a/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc.c	2026-08-27 14:58:25.299267010 -0400
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc.c	2026-08-27 14:58:25.300177951 -0400
@@ -213,11 +213,26 @@
 	 */
 	if ((cmd_flags & SDMMC_CMD_PRV_DAT_WAIT) &&
 	    !(cmd_flags & SDMMC_CMD_VOLT_SWITCH)) {
+		/*
+		 * An all-ones read means the controller is not answering at all
+		 * - unclocked, powered down, or the card has fallen off the bus
+		 * - it does not mean "busy". Treating it as busy makes this
+		 * atomic poll burn its full 500ms with host->lock held and
+		 * interrupts disabled, and a caller that keeps retrying then
+		 * pins the CPU: interrupts stop being serviced, RCU stalls, and
+		 * the machine wedges hard enough that printk stops coming out.
+		 * Break out instead and let the command time out normally, so
+		 * the error reaches the driver and its recovery path can run.
+		 */
 		if (readl_poll_timeout_atomic(host->regs + SDMMC_STATUS,
 					      status,
+					      status == ~0U ||
 					      !(status & SDMMC_STATUS_BUSY),
 					      10, 500 * USEC_PER_MSEC))
 			dev_err(host->dev, "Busy; trying anyway\n");
+		else if (status == ~0U)
+			dev_err_ratelimited(host->dev,
+					    "host not responding (status=~0)\n");
 	}
 }
 
